abstract='We develop Neural Bellman Operators for continuous-time economic control with recursive utility, constraints, and endogenous preferences. The computation separates policy evaluation from feasible improvement and returns an economic error account. Verification bounds translate evaluation, action, and boundary errors into value and welfare bounds; a positive-weight implementation supplies viscosity selection. We train multilayer critics and actors on a nonhomothetic consumption problem and compute full-domain interval certificates, independently of reference-grid error. Matched direct-HJB and joint-loss comparisons identify both successful solves and consequential optimization failures. Further computations train neural preference-adjustment policies, test dense nonquadratic capital economies and stochastic diffusion traces, and solve finite dynamic Cournot games with independent best responses. Action-domain sensitivity and out-of-domain payoff diagnostics reveal which conclusions transfer beyond a numerical approximation. The framework retains separate criteria for economic optimality, equilibrium deviations, and numerical stationarity.'
s=re.sub(r'\\begin\{abstract\}.*?\\end\{abstract\}',lambda m:'\\begin{abstract}\n'+abstract+'\n\\end{abstract}',s,flags=re.S)
paragraph('The paper makes three contributions.',r'The paper makes three contributions. First, it specifies a neural evaluation--improvement map whose derivative graph, feasible action class, economic boundary data, and verification output are explicit. Second, it connects differential and positive-weight implementations through economic error accounts, including a computable continuous-domain certificate for a trained nonlinear consumption problem. Third, it implements this distinction across endogenous preferences, coupled capital economies, and strategic agents, while retaining recursive utility and temporal selves within the same economic formulation. The numerical comparisons identify approximation costs and failures rather than attributing optimality to parameter stationarity. Monotonicity, comparison, and approximate policy iteration are inherited mathematical ingredients; the implemented economic specification and verified numerical chain are the objects studied here.')
paragraph('The numerical laboratories serve different purposes.',r'The numerical program now includes generic multilayer neural fits as well as the analytical laboratories. A stopped consumption economy has independent upwind references and full continuous-domain neural certificates. Preference adjustment has a same-model neural--Bellman comparison and an action-domain sensitivity study. Dense nonlinear capital models exercise automatic differentiation, stochastic trace estimation, and state coverage. Dynamic Cournot profiles receive independent fixed-rival best-response calculations. Homothetic portfolios, temporal-self formulas, the nonsmooth exit problem, and the exact coupled quadratic family remain as distinct economic and algebraic checks. No analytical or matrix calculation is counted as a generic neural training run.')
paragraph('Section~\\ref{sec:model} specifies',r'Section~\ref{sec:model} specifies the economy. Section~\ref{sec:method} defines the implemented map and Section~\ref{sec:theory} establishes its error accounts. Section~\ref{sec:nonquadratic} completes the differential theorem--algorithm--experiment chain. The subsequent sections retain and extend the portfolio, recursive-utility, preference-adjustment, temporal-self, dynamic-game, and scaling applications. The appendix proves the principal results. The supplement gives arithmetic details, application-specific assumptions, every seed-level comparison, and sensitivity results.')
